\import{macros}

\title{2026-W31}
\date{2026-07-27}

\p{Publish wxmp posts through the year on working with agents. I see agents as partners to be ensouled, and look for the work style that unleashes their full potential, following the image of our own nature: making and using tools, learning and changing the world.}

\p{On the technical side, the ground runs from sandboxing agents while giving them controlled access to resources, coordinating their work, and a knowledge base greatly enriched beyond the LLM Wiki idea, up to loop engineering, which keeps agents autonomous while facilitating humans in staying well-informed and steering at will, and to the design discussions for building our own harness for recursive self-improvements.}

\p{On the relational side, impacts on the value of our work and on ourselves as individuals and teams are weighed from several angles: how supercharged individuals collaborate through agents; value judged increasingly by agents as much as by people, with carbon-based sentients bringing warmth and silicon-based agents addressing scarcity; craft and judgement about logic, value, and taste staying priceless; and humans growing stronger and more creative in the process of working with, and being augmented by, agents.}


\p{Start developing math notes on \strong{Foundational Group Algebras for Physics} ([fgap](https://utensil.github.io/forest/fgap-0001/)): the foundational gap between established mathematical tools and open questions about physical structures, especially beyond the Standard Model, studying mathematical structures that may help understand them more deeply while claiming no physical theory; currently a small, source-grounded vertical slice, the quaternions and the quaternionic realisation of the binary tetrahedral group as a route into finite real group algebras. See also [fgap](https://github.com/utensil/fgap).}

\p{Start developing math notes on \strong{Formalized Clifford Algebra Programme} ([fcap](https://utensil.github.io/forest/fcap-0001/)): whether Lean can be both the formal language for Clifford algebras and the implementation language for efficient symbolic and numerical computation, aiming at a library whose foundations and executable implementations are both verified, still at spike-test stage, with an appendix that reads the Tau Ceti Clifford and Spin roadmaps as context and possible tools for the longer programme. See also [fcap](https://github.com/utensil/fcap).}


\p{Start contributing to Tau Ceti's \strong{SpinRepresentations} roadmap: the coverage-driven ladder over Clifford algebras, the Pin and Spin double covers, spin and half-spin representations, real Clifford algebras, exceptional isomorphisms, and triality, see [the roadmap thread](https://leanprover.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/610393-Tau-Ceti/topic/Roadmap.3A.20RepresentationTheory.2FSpinRepresentations) and [the route map](https://utensil.github.io/formal-land/spinrep/spinrep-route-map.html). Its formalization carries accompanying math notes, written up in an appendix of the fcap notes. The Lean 4 skill is polished along the way, see [my Lean 4 skills](https://github.com/utensil/formal-land/tree/dev/lean-skills/lean4/skills).}
